Of course. As a code expert, I will provide a comprehensive, professional solution for a client-side Karaoke Lyrics Synchronization Tool. This tool will be built using pure HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, requiring no server-side setup, making it easy to run and demonstrate.

### Professional Overview

This tool provides a complete solution for karaoke enthusiasts and content creators to synchronize lyrics with audio. It addresses the core requirements of loading media, displaying synchronized lyrics with word-by-word precision, adjusting timing globally, and exporting the results. The architecture is modular and client-side, ensuring privacy and performance.

### Key Features

1.  **Audio & LRC Upload:** Users can upload local audio files (MP3, WAV, etc.) and LRC lyrics files.
2.  **Synchronized Display:** Lyrics are displayed and automatically scroll with the audio playback.
3.  **Word-by-Word Highlighting:** The tool supports an **enhanced LRC format** for precise, word-level highlighting, a feature common in professional karaoke systems. It gracefully degrades to line-by-line highlighting for standard LRC files.
4.  **Timing Offset Adjustment:** A global offset can be applied in real-time to correct synchronization issues, shifting all timestamps forward or backward.
5.  **Modified LRC Export:** The newly timed LRC file, with the offset applied, can be exported for future use.

---

### The Enhanced LRC Format

Standard LRC provides timestamps for each line. For word-by-word highlighting, we need timestamps for each word. We will use a widely adopted enhanced format where each word is preceded by its own timestamp.

**Standard LRC Line:**
`[00:12.34]This is a line of lyrics`

**Enhanced LRC Line (for word-by-word sync):**
`[00:12.34]<00:12.34>This <00:12.80>is <00:13.10>a <00:13.50>line <00:13.95>of <00:14.30>lyrics`

Our parser will intelligently detect this format. If word timestamps (`<mm:ss.xx>`) are not present, it will fall back to line-by-line highlighting.

